{
 "text": " What if an iron bar shot straight through your head? September 1848, Vermont. Phineas Gage, 25, is blasting rock for a new railroad. He packs gunpowder into a hole with an iron rod about a meter long, 6 kilos. A spark sets it off. The rod rockets up through his left cheek, behind his eye, and out the top of his skull. It lands 25 meters away. And Phineas? He's awake. Within minutes he's talking. He survives. But his friends say he has changed. Rude, restless, swearing. They say he's no longer gauge. His case helps scientists link the front of the brain to who we are. Phineas moves to Chile and drives stagecoaches for about seven years. He lives 12 more years after the accident. Today, his skull and the rod sit side by side in a Harvard museum.",
